## The PDF is stamped, not rebuilt

The form is a flat A4 PDF with no AcroForm fields. We load it as a template and draw
values on top with `pdf-lib`; we do **not** reconstruct the layout in HTML/CSS and we
do **not** alter the form's own text.

This is a correctness requirement, not an aesthetic one. ITL 054 is a standardised
fiscal document filed with the local tax authority — a form that does not match the
official one can be refused at the counter. It follows that:

- No clause may be added to the form. The declarations the parties make (the vehicle
  is the seller's property and free of encumbrances; keys, registration certificate
  and CIV changed hands; the price was paid; both parties know the Criminal Code
  provisions on forgery) are already printed at **section (5)**.
- If a "sold as seen, no further claims" clause is wanted, it belongs in a **separate
  annex** — the form even has an "Anexe la contract: Da / Nu" tick box for it.
- Cartuşe **A, B, C and D** and the signature lines are left blank. Those are filled
  by the tax authority and by hand, respectively.

Coordinates live in a generated, committed `pdf/field-map.json`. The running service
reads that JSON — it must never re-parse the template PDF per request.

Diacritics: the PDF standard fonts have no ă/â/î/ș/ț, so a TTF is embedded. Times New
Roman cannot be redistributed, so DejaVu Serif (from the `dejavu-fonts-ttf` package)
is used for the filled-in values. The form's own text is untouched either way.

## Personal data: how this module treats it

This is the only part of the backend that handles **CNPs, ID card series/numbers and
home addresses** — and for the *other* party to the sale, who is typically not a user
of this app and never agreed to anything with us. The rules below are not optional
polish; they are why the feature can ship.

1. **Identity photos are never stored.** `POST /sale-contract/extract-identity` uses
   `memoryStorage()`, passes the buffer to the AI provider, returns suggestions and
   drops it. Nothing reaches the disk or the bucket.
2. **Identity data is encrypted before it reaches Postgres.** Each party's whole
   payload is one AES-256-GCM blob in `SaleContractParty.data_encrypted`, via
   `FieldEncryptionService`. Nothing identifying is stored outside the blob — not even
   a masked name — so the table alone yields no personal data.
3. **Nothing personal is ever logged.** Not at debug level, not inside an error
   message. Use `redact()` / `redactText()` from `common/crypto` before logging
   anything that has been near a party payload or a provider response.
4. **Contracts are not shared.** Access is by ownership, never by car access: sharing
   a car with someone was never consent to show them a third party's CNP.
5. **Retention is enforced by a job, not by good intentions.**
   `SaleContractRetentionService` runs daily and erases the parties' data and the
   stored PDF once `retention_until` passes, leaving only the non-identifying vehicle,
   price and date so the car's history keeps its shape. Default 90 days; the user can
   opt in to keep the contract, which extends it — but still bounds it.
6. **Delete means delete.** `DELETE /sale-contract/:id` removes the row and the stored
   object. No soft delete: a row that still holds an encrypted CNP has not been
   deleted.
7. **Consent is recorded.** Reading the other party's document requires stating which
   version of the consent wording was shown; it is stored with a timestamp.

### Why identity extraction has its own AI provider

The rest of the app extracts documents with Gemini on the **free** tier, whose terms
allow the provider to use submitted data to improve their products. That is acceptable
for an insurance policy photo and unacceptable for an identity card. Identity
extraction therefore runs through a separate, **paid** provider behind
`IdentityExtractionProvider`, selected by env, so the two never mix.

A CNP read off a photo is checked against its control digit (`common/crypto/cnp.util`).
A failing checksum does not discard the value — it downgrades confidence and adds a
warning, because a single misread digit is the likeliest failure here and produces a
contract that is invalid at the counter while looking perfectly fine on screen.

## The confirmations are for us, not for the form

`POST /sale-contract/:id/generate` requires four confirmations, each of which must be
literally `true` or the request is rejected: the data was verified, the section (5)
declarations were read, the Criminal Code note was acknowledged, and the user
understands the app generates a form rather than giving legal advice.

They are stored with a timestamp on the contract. They never appear on the PDF. Their
purpose is evidence of what the user was shown and agreed to — protection for us as
the tool's provider; they are not a substitute for the parties' handwritten signatures.

## Environment

| Variable | Required | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `CONTRACT_ENCRYPTION_KEY` | yes | base64, decodes to exactly 32 bytes. Without it the module's encryption refuses to run. |
| `CONTRACT_RETENTION_DAYS` | no (90) | Default retention window. |
| `CONTRACT_KEPT_RETENTION_DAYS` | no (1825) | Window when the user opts to keep the contract. |
| `IDENTITY_EXTRACTION_PROVIDER` | no | `cloudflare` (the intended vendor, and the default when both are configured) or `claude` (kept wired up as the comparison arm for the accuracy A/B). |
| `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` | per provider | Claude adapter. |
| `ANTHROPIC_IDENTITY_MODEL` | no | Defaults to `claude-haiku-4-5`. |
| `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` / `CLOUDFLARE_AI_TOKEN` / `CLOUDFLARE_VISION_MODEL` | per provider | Workers AI adapter. |
| `CLOUDFLARE_AI_GATEWAY_URL` | no | If used, **disable log storage on the gateway** — it logs requests and responses by default, which would mean a copy of every identity document. |

Missing keys disable the relevant capability with a warning rather than crashing
bootstrap, matching `GeminiExtractionService` — the dev checkout is shared with the
test server, so a hard startup requirement risks taking down unrelated environments.

## Endpoints

All under `JwtAuthGuard`, all scoped to the signed-in user.

| Method | Path | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| POST | `/sale-contract/extract-identity` | multipart; suggestions only, stores nothing |
| GET | `/sale-contract` | summaries, no personal data, no decryption |
| POST | `/sale-contract` | create draft |
| GET | `/sale-contract/:id` | full, decrypted, owner only |
| PUT | `/sale-contract/:id` | update draft |
| POST | `/sale-contract/:id/generate` | confirmations required; returns a signed link |
| GET | `/sale-contract/:id/download` | fresh signed link |
| DELETE | `/sale-contract/:id` | hard delete, including the stored PDF |
| GET/PUT/DELETE | `/sale-contract/my-identity` | the user's own saved details |

**Route order matters**: `my-identity` is declared before the `:id` routes, or Nest
matches it as an id and the request dies in `ParseIntPipe`.

## Schema notes

`prisma db push` is how this schema reaches an environment (see the `db:push:*`
scripts), matching how the other recent models were added.

`SaleContract.status` gains `PURGED`, which means "this sale happened, and its personal
data has been erased" — distinct from a contract that was deleted outright.
